WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.760
Why are some languages
more difficult than others?

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.710
It's a subject that comes up all the time.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.770
I want to talk about that today, and
then I'm going to introduce you to a

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.430
very interesting person, where we
discuss the subject in greater detail.

00:00:11.435 --> 00:00:15.450
I think there are six or seven
major reasons why we find learning

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.270
languages difficult, and some
more difficult than others.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.880
The first reason, I think, and one
of the biggest reasons, is that we

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.590
put too much pressure on ourselves.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.600
It's difficult to learn a language.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.390
In other words, it's more
difficult than speaking your

00:00:27.405 --> 00:00:28.245
own language.

00:00:28.275 --> 00:00:31.155
When you speak your own language,
there is no pressure, and even

00:00:31.155 --> 00:00:35.235
there you have a much larger passive
vocabulary than active vocabulary.

00:00:35.295 --> 00:00:38.295
Point number two then is the
more different the language

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.265
is from your own language, the
more difficult it's going to be.

00:00:41.295 --> 00:00:45.225
Obviously a different writing system or
similar vocabulary, similar structure.

00:00:45.255 --> 00:00:48.555
One example, which I'll show you,
is when I go to do Dutch, it's

00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:51.345
very easy for me because it's
written in the Latin alphabet.

00:00:51.345 --> 00:00:51.885
It has a lot of

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:55.720
common vocabulary with languages that
I already speak. So it's a breeze.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.130
Let's take the example of Dutch.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.110
I decided to play around a bit with Dutch.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.490
I hadn't been studying Dutch.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.530
I went in there [in LingQ] and I did the first
five lessons in Sentence View, and

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:09.610
I just breezed through it in no
time, even though the Dutch shows

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.850
me that there are 79% new words.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:16.120
In fact, the language is so
similar to languages that I know

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.590
that I can go through it in

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.520
Sentence View, understand it, and
pretty soon I'm getting a flavor of

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.050
Dutch and I feel I'm getting somewhere.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:27.390
If I go now to Arabic, for the same
percentage of unknown words, even

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.290
this, it says 12% unknown words, I know
it's gonna be super difficult for me.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.920
It's not just the new words, it's
also how similar the language is to

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.539
a language that you already know.

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:40.320
The third reason is I think people
take on too much at one time.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:42.855
That's why we have Sentence Mode at LingQ.

00:01:42.855 --> 00:01:46.785
You know, reduce the amount of strange
new words that you are looking at.

00:01:46.785 --> 00:01:48.825
Help yourself as much as possible.

00:01:48.854 --> 00:01:51.134
Listen to it, make it less strange.

00:01:51.134 --> 00:01:52.455
Take it in smaller bites.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:56.205
A fourth reason is that certain
languages have sort of quirks.

00:01:56.205 --> 00:01:59.085
It might be the spelling
system in English, or even

00:01:59.115 --> 00:02:00.615
in French, or even in Greek.

00:02:00.675 --> 00:02:06.164
Or it might be gender, or it might be the
case system in German or Slavic languages.

00:02:06.164 --> 00:02:10.185
So these particular quirks can make some
languages more difficult than others.

00:02:10.285 --> 00:02:13.945
The fifth reason is the availability
of interesting content.

00:02:14.125 --> 00:02:17.515
I think when we start, we have a
high tolerance for kind of repetitive

00:02:17.515 --> 00:02:21.715
and somewhat boring material, but
we have to then get onto things of

00:02:21.745 --> 00:02:26.484
interest and some languages just have
more availability, like English, like

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:30.655
Spanish, like Russian, of that kind
of interesting content at our level.

00:02:30.715 --> 00:02:33.775
Another big factor is
your inherent motivation.

00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:35.725
So a language may be difficult.

00:02:35.745 --> 00:02:39.045
Intrinsically, for all the reasons that
I've pointed out, it's more different.

00:02:39.045 --> 00:02:42.075
It has these quirks, call it.
There's a lack of resources.

00:02:42.075 --> 00:02:44.595
But if you're tremendously
motivated, you can overcome those.

00:02:44.595 --> 00:02:47.895
So let's make that just sort of a
brief introduction to this subject.

00:02:47.925 --> 00:02:51.105
And now I want you to
meet Zoe of Zoe Languages.

00:02:51.105 --> 00:02:54.285
And we discussed this in
greater detail. So it's very

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.105
appropriate that you are from China.

00:02:57.135 --> 00:02:59.895
So many people consider
Chinese to be very difficult.

00:02:59.925 --> 00:03:01.905
I was recently in Poland, the

00:03:01.955 --> 00:03:04.475
Polls consider their language
to be very difficult.

00:03:04.475 --> 00:03:07.175
The Koreans say their language
is the most difficult.

00:03:07.235 --> 00:03:09.785
I'm having trouble with Arabic right now.

00:03:09.815 --> 00:03:12.875
I know that you speak Persian
and Arabic, and of course you're

00:03:12.875 --> 00:03:14.075
a native speaker of Chinese.

00:03:14.135 --> 00:03:17.765
From your perspective, what is the
most difficult language to learn?

00:03:18.065 --> 00:03:18.395
Okay.

00:03:18.395 --> 00:03:19.746
Firstly, I want to thank you.

00:03:19.995 --> 00:03:22.955
I want to say that I was inspired by you.

00:03:23.135 --> 00:03:27.605
I've been watching a lot of your
videos, and after learning Arabic, I've

00:03:27.605 --> 00:03:29.435
decided to learn Turkish and Persian.

00:03:29.585 --> 00:03:30.335
Because of you!

00:03:30.335 --> 00:03:31.715
Because you have inspired me.

00:03:31.715 --> 00:03:31.775
Wow.

00:03:31.805 --> 00:03:32.795
I want to say thank you.

00:03:33.725 --> 00:03:34.055
Yeah.

00:03:34.115 --> 00:03:36.785
Do you know that FSI Ranking
Foreign Service Institute?

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:37.805
Exactly.

00:03:38.015 --> 00:03:41.255
This famous ranking is
geared towards English

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.070
speakers.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:42.280
Mm-hmm.

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:44.620
It varies depending on your mother tongue.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:49.090
Some people will still insist there's no
such thing as the most difficult language.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:49.180
Right.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.310
They argue it's Russian or it's is Greek.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.350
I think you have more experience than me.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.880
Also, some people even say English,
as they have been learning it for so

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:02.709
long, but still can't speak it. And
their points are not entirely off-base.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.269
For example, I speak seven languages.

00:04:05.535 --> 00:04:08.385
I can't count Mandarin because
it's my native language.

00:04:08.475 --> 00:04:11.655
I think the most difficult
foreign language for me is

00:04:11.655 --> 00:04:13.454
Arabic. It's my number one.

00:04:13.545 --> 00:04:13.905
I've been

00:04:14.175 --> 00:04:14.865
struggling.

00:04:14.865 --> 00:04:17.175
Can you give us a list of
the languages that you speak?

00:04:17.505 --> 00:04:18.015
I'll give you the

00:04:18.015 --> 00:04:18.404
list.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:22.275
According to the difficulty.
The first one, number one is Arabic.

00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:25.094
My native language is Mandarin,
right, and the most difficult

00:04:25.094 --> 00:04:26.865
language I've ever learned is Arabic.

00:04:26.925 --> 00:04:28.275
The second one is German.

00:04:28.545 --> 00:04:30.255
The third one is Turkish.

00:04:30.255 --> 00:04:33.195
The fourth one is French,
and then the next is Persian.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.650
The last one is English.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:34.799
Okay.

00:04:34.799 --> 00:04:37.770
But this list isn't objective at all.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:41.400
It's completely relative, and based on
my personal learning journey. When we

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.419
talk about difficulty, we can have
difficulty in speaking correctly.

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:46.890
In other words, complicated grammar.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:50.969
We can have difficulty in
pronunciation. We can have difficulty

00:04:50.974 --> 00:04:54.990
in comprehension so that the aspects
of the language that are difficult

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.700
can vary from language to language.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:57.390
Would you agree?

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:58.500
Totally agree.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:02.820
But I prefer to say that it's easy
to define the difficulty of learning

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.550
the basics of a language.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.340
It's straightforward to identify how
hard the pronunciation is, for example,

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.850
like the four tones in Mandarin,
Chinese, and or the writing system

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.830
and how complex the grammar rules are.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:18.750
Because, for example, Arabic, because of
the gender, and a lot of other grammatical

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.620
rules like German. Chinese tones
and writings are very, very difficult.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.719
But these kind of things
can be learned in practice.

00:05:25.719 --> 00:05:30.070
But in my opinion, once we reach
an upper-intermediate level, all

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:35.049
languages become equally challenging
because the difficulty lies elsewhere.

00:05:35.049 --> 00:05:36.640
It's in the immersion environment.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:39.669
Your knowledge of the
culture and society and so on.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:42.010
Here, I would like to give an example.

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:44.979
In my opinion, Persian
would be easier than Arabic.

00:05:44.995 --> 00:05:48.475
In terms of the grammar in
Arabic, there are complex gender,

00:05:48.475 --> 00:05:50.395
plural, and conjugation rules.

00:05:50.545 --> 00:05:54.085
However, when I travel to Iran
and communicate with locals,

00:05:54.295 --> 00:05:57.055
I find it easy to have a basic
conversation, to express myself.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.445
I learned very quickly at the beginner
level until the intermediate level.

00:06:00.655 --> 00:06:05.245
I already have a lot of vocabulary
because I knew Arabic already

00:06:05.515 --> 00:06:10.255
and the grammar is easier than Arabic.
But while I was in Iran, sometimes I

00:06:10.255 --> 00:06:14.395
can even discuss complex
topics because we have the context.

00:06:14.725 --> 00:06:17.035
Of the conversation we are interacting.

00:06:17.305 --> 00:06:20.935
People are aware that I'm a
foreigner, so they speak slower.

00:06:20.935 --> 00:06:24.595
Use simple vocabulary and
clear sentence structure

00:06:24.595 --> 00:06:25.795
to help me understand better.

00:06:25.885 --> 00:06:30.475
However, it is still extremely challenging
for me to follow my Iranian friends'

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:31.390
conversations.

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.750
They speak quickly and use a
lot of colloquial expressions.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:38.650
So understanding them requires
knowledge of culture and the social

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:39.160
context.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.890
I certainly do agree with you that
Persian is easier than Arabic.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:44.410
I wouldn't have put German
as such a difficult language,

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.090
but that's your experience.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:48.670
Someone who's native language
is English, maybe has an easier

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:49.720
time with German. I don't know.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:54.370
But another aspect of difficulty
is how easy it is to find good

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:55.890
learning content.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.390
Interesting learning content.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:00.720
Yeah, so, generally speaking, it's
becoming easier to find good learning

00:07:00.725 --> 00:07:02.700
content nowadays via the internet.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:07.110
I was just, before our discussion,
I went to MTV Lebanon and I got

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.990
an MP3 file from there, and
I can import that into LingQ and

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:11.700
it automatically transcribes it.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.710
And so I have a lesson that
wouldn't have been possible

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:17.120
even a few years ago. [For] Persian,
it's more difficult to find good

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.830
learning content. In some languages,

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:22.159
there's just an abundance of
good audio and text learning content.

00:07:22.159 --> 00:07:22.219
Yeah.

00:07:22.219 --> 00:07:24.890
So that's another factor
that affects difficulty.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:25.039
Yeah.

00:07:25.045 --> 00:07:25.140
Yeah.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:25.760
Agree.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:30.169
That's why I think it's not really fair
to put English at the end of my list.

00:07:30.289 --> 00:07:32.090
We already learn English at school.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:32.270
Mm-hmm.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:35.599
And these days, we expose
ourselves to English content.

00:07:35.599 --> 00:07:38.719
As you said, it's easy to
find immersion environments.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.890
Which make our studies seem easier
compared to languages that lack learning

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:45.170
resources such as Persian or Turkish.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:50.270
So I personally don't find English
difficult, not surprisingly, but

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.630
it also depends. You know, some
people say, "Well, English is easy!"

00:07:53.635 --> 00:07:56.180
It is easier because
of the environment.

00:07:56.185 --> 00:07:58.490
So many people speak English,
whether they're native speakers

00:07:58.490 --> 00:07:59.510
or not native speakers.

00:07:59.515 --> 00:07:59.560
Yeah.

00:07:59.805 --> 00:08:03.195
Now then the question is, how well
do you want to speak this language?

00:08:03.225 --> 00:08:06.105
There are people, for example, who say
that French is hard to understand because

00:08:06.105 --> 00:08:10.635
of the liaison, because it's hard to hear
the clear sort of definition of where

00:08:10.635 --> 00:08:12.555
one word ends and the next word begins.

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.405
Japanese is somehow
difficult because of the way

00:08:15.405 --> 00:08:16.755
the Japanese people communicate.

00:08:16.755 --> 00:08:19.755
There's a lot of things that are not
said that are kind of understood.

00:08:19.784 --> 00:08:22.335
So I think every language
comes with its difficulty.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.015
To me, the biggest issue is motivation.

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:26.430
If you're very motivated,
difficulties fade away.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:27.150
Agree.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.920
I have a question for you, actually.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:29.070
Yeah.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:30.300
You know 20

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:31.830
languages, right?

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:35.250
Not always up to the level I'd like,
but experience learning 20 languages.

00:08:35.255 --> 00:08:35.549
Yes.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:36.179
I'm curious

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:39.480
about your ranking, the difficulty
ranking of these languages.

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:39.780
Well,

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.000
right now I would put Arabic at the top.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.390
If I say, okay, Chinese, the
difficulty there is the characters.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.250
There's... that's just a matter of time.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.885
You have to put in so much time
because, you know, the sooner you learn a

00:08:50.885 --> 00:08:53.765
character than you forget it. And
you gotta learn it again and again.

00:08:53.765 --> 00:08:54.905
And so that takes a lot of time.

00:08:54.935 --> 00:08:57.935
On the other hand, when I was learning
Chinese, it was my full-time job, so

00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:59.825
I was doing it six, seven hours a day.

00:08:59.855 --> 00:09:02.615
So within a year I was
reading books in Chinese.

00:09:02.675 --> 00:09:05.195
The grammar in Chinese is extremely easy.

00:09:05.285 --> 00:09:08.555
It's probably the easiest grammar
of any language I've ever learned.

00:09:08.555 --> 00:09:09.865
Nothing changes.

00:09:09.925 --> 00:09:12.535
There's no gender, there's no conjugation.

00:09:12.594 --> 00:09:15.265
There's some patterns of how the
language works, and you get used

00:09:15.265 --> 00:09:16.615
to those patterns and away you go.

00:09:16.675 --> 00:09:18.834
So Arabic certainly is
up there in difficulty.

00:09:18.834 --> 00:09:22.405
I think the Slavic languages are
difficult because of a very complicated

00:09:22.405 --> 00:09:25.854
grammar, so many different changes to
words, depending on their function

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:25.944
Mm-hmm.

00:09:26.189 --> 00:09:29.515
in a sentence. I never consider
pronunciation a big issue.

00:09:29.545 --> 00:09:32.275
If I pronounce it well or less
well, it's good enough.

00:09:32.275 --> 00:09:33.655
You know, I don't worry about that.

00:09:33.685 --> 00:09:37.194
But it's hard to speak the
Slavic languages accurately,

00:09:37.255 --> 00:09:38.005
in other words, correctly.

00:09:38.305 --> 00:09:38.425
Mm-hmm.

00:09:38.775 --> 00:09:42.405
In Chinese, it's hard to make a
mistake, assuming you have the word.

00:09:42.405 --> 00:09:45.795
And of course, another advantage
in Chinese is if you get the

00:09:45.824 --> 00:09:49.814
characters, you can easily build your
vocabulary because so many of what we

00:09:49.814 --> 00:09:53.625
would call words in English are in
fact the combination of characters.

00:09:53.714 --> 00:09:56.655
So the more characters you
have, the more new words you can

00:09:56.655 --> 00:09:58.334
learn or recognize or guess at.

00:09:58.365 --> 00:10:00.255
No, I would say that the difficulty right now

00:10:00.255 --> 00:10:01.574
is Arabic for sure. Agree.

00:10:02.564 --> 00:10:04.245
And that's about it.

00:10:04.245 --> 00:10:06.765
The easiest to learn is any
extension of a language you

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:08.324
know; another romance language.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:12.030
Another Slavic language. Korean
I found difficult because of a lack

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:15.810
of interesting content with audio
and text that I could study on LingQ.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:18.690
See, I'm not so interested
in series on Netflix.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.640
K-Drama or K-Pop.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.710
No, not that doesn't interest me.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.620
But to those people who are
interested in that, then there's a

00:10:25.625 --> 00:10:27.090
wealth of material for learning Korean.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:27.540
Yeah.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:27.810
So.

00:10:28.175 --> 00:10:31.595
It's very subjective, but I'm very
impressed that you have reached

00:10:31.595 --> 00:10:35.525
out to learn languages of different
language groups, and I think it's

00:10:35.525 --> 00:10:38.375
an exciting thing to do to explore
different parts of the world.

00:10:38.375 --> 00:10:41.255
Now you're exploring the Middle
East, which I'm also trying to do.

00:10:41.255 --> 00:10:42.515
I was inspired by you!

00:10:42.995 --> 00:10:47.585
Let's hope that we inspire people who
are watching this to not spend too much

00:10:47.585 --> 00:10:50.915
time worrying about the difficulty, be
motivated by their interests, whether it

00:10:50.915 --> 00:10:53.075
be history and geography, as is our case.

00:10:53.355 --> 00:10:57.555
Or anime for Japanese or K-pop
for Korean or whatever it might

00:10:57.555 --> 00:11:01.575
be for Chinese or other languages,
and go out and explore the world.

00:11:01.575 --> 00:11:02.895
It's a wonderful thing to do.

00:11:02.955 --> 00:11:03.105
Yeah.

00:11:03.135 --> 00:11:04.755
Well listen, thank you very much.

00:11:04.755 --> 00:11:04.835
Thank you.

00:11:04.835 --> 00:11:10.665
And you are @Zoe.Languages on YouTube
and people can go there and watch you

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:12.015
explore these different languages.

00:11:12.015 --> 00:11:15.825
I saw your videos when you were in
Iran, conversing with the people there

00:11:15.825 --> 00:11:17.505
in the shops or with your friends.

00:11:17.595 --> 00:11:19.575
A wonderful example for us to follow.

00:11:19.575 --> 00:11:20.685
Thank you for joining me.

